  "evidences": [
    {
      "id": "ev_12",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "VirusBulletin_EvronRaz(10-05-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s12"
      },
      "text": "Some of the most notable breaches and hacks in the past few years have been a result of APT campaigns, such as Stuxnet and Flame.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_14",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "VirusBulletin_EvronRaz(10-05-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s1"
      },
      "text": "example is Gauss, which had tight execution constraints which only allowed the malware to execute on certain targets, resulting in it evading capture for signiﬁ cantly longer.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_16",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "VirusBulletin_EvronRaz(10-05-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s3"
      },
      "text": "Another APT that emerged as the actors learned from their mistakes was APT3, a piece of malware that, when ﬁ rst active, used only small and disposable tools before bringing out the more complex and intense tools, in order to prevent their loss in the event of it being caught earlier on.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_17",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "VirusBulletin_EvronRaz(10-05-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s4"
      },
      "text": "Its disclosure of the advanced persistent threat campaign named ‘APT1’ made a huge impact thanks to the extent of the exposure.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_19",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "VirusBulletin_EvronRaz(10-05-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s6"
      },
      "text": "With a sizable staff, APT1 was able to steal incredible amounts of data, remaining in one particular system for as long as four years and 10 months, and stealing a whopping 6.5 Terabytes of data from just one organization over the course of 10 months.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_20",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "VirusBulletin_EvronRaz(10-05-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s7"
      },
      "text": "Mandiant ’s report outed APT1 publicly for the ﬁ rst time and, since then, APT reports have become a common outcome after an attack, produced with the intent of aiding other defenders by detailing the attack methods and preventative measures.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_28",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "VirusBulletin_EvronRaz(10-05-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s15"
      },
      "text": "In the case of APT1, different parts of the malware were registered under the same name and email.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_34",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "VirusBulletin_EvronRaz(10-05-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p3_s5"
      },
      "text": "This can be seen in the case of the threat known as ‘Hurricane Panda’ where the threat actor detected the presence of CrowdStrike and backed away.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_35",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "VirusBulletin_EvronRaz(10-05-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p3_s6"
      },
      "text": "Some fold after report publications: Red October was a malware program operating for ﬁ ve years before its discovery, upon which it dismantled.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_36",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "VirusBulletin_EvronRaz(10-05-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p3_s7"
      },
      "text": "Nevertheless, there are counter-examples that demonstrate that some malware actors do not bother to fold and retreat, such as APT12 or the Gaza Hacker team.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
